## Patent Disclosure Summary: Components and Methods for CVT Shift Control

### Technical Field

The disclosure relates generally to continuously variable transmissions (“CVTs”) and, more particularly, to mechanical shift-control systems for regulating the ratio-changing elements of a traction-type CVT. The disclosed subject matter includes feedback cam mechanisms, skew cam mechanisms, carrier plate arrangements, neutralizer assemblies, and cooperative structures associated with a traction sun or sun element for controlling, stabilizing, and neutralizing CVT shifting.

### Background

Traction-type CVTs commonly vary transmission ratio by changing the angular orientation, skew condition, or contact geometry of traction elements disposed between input and output race structures. Accurate shift control is important for achieving smooth ratio change, stable operation at steady ratio, and predictable response to operator or actuator commands.

Conventional CVT controls may require complex hydraulic, electronic, or servo systems to position ratio-changing members. In some arrangements, hysteresis, overshoot, backlash, or insufficient mechanical feedback may adversely affect ratio stability. There is therefore a need for compact mechanical arrangements that provide controlled skew input, feedback correction, centering or neutralization, and cooperative interaction with the traction sun or related ratio-control member.

### Summary of Disclosure

Disclosed are components and methods for controlling shift movement in a CVT through coordinated operation of one or more feedback cams, skew cams, carrier plates, neutralizer assemblies, and traction sun cooperating features. The control system may convert an actuator input into a controlled skew or tilt command applied to traction elements, while feedback generated from transmission ratio position or traction sun displacement reduces or cancels the command as a target ratio is approached.

In one aspect, a shift-control assembly includes a movable carrier plate supporting or engaging a ratio-control component of the CVT. A skew cam is arranged to impart a skewing force, displacement, or angular bias to the carrier plate or to a traction element support. A feedback cam is arranged to oppose, modify, or terminate the skew command in response to an actual ratio condition. A neutralizer assembly may bias the control mechanism toward a neutral or non-shifting condition, thereby stabilizing the CVT when no ratio change is commanded.

In another aspect, the traction sun cooperates with the carrier plate and cam mechanisms to provide a mechanical feedback path. Movement of the traction sun, or a component coupled thereto, may drive a feedback cam surface that changes the effective position of a cam follower, control link, carrier plate, skew ring, or related element. The feedback relationship may cause the skew command to decay as the actual ratio approaches a commanded ratio.

### Representative Components

#### 1. Feedback Cam

A feedback cam may include one or more profiled cam surfaces configured to generate a displacement, force, or angular correction proportional to CVT ratio position or traction sun position. The feedback cam may be rotatable, translatable, oscillating, or otherwise movable relative to a housing or carrier member.

The feedback cam may cooperate with a cam follower, roller, pin, shoe, ball, ramp follower, linkage end, or bearing element. The follower may transmit feedback movement to a carrier plate, skew cam, neutralizer member, control lever, or actuator interface.

In representative embodiments, the feedback cam may:

- provide negative feedback to reduce shift-command error;
- define a predetermined ratio-control curve;
- compensate for nonlinear traction geometry;
- provide end-stop or ratio-limit functions;
- bias the system toward steady-state operation at a selected ratio;
- cooperate with a traction sun member to sense actual ratio position; and/or
- provide hysteresis reduction by maintaining follower preload.

#### 2. Skew Cam

A skew cam may be configured to generate a skew angle, tilt bias, or lateral offset that initiates ratio change in the CVT. The skew cam may include inclined ramps, helical surfaces, eccentric surfaces, curved slots, wedge faces, or other cam features.

The skew cam may be actuated by a mechanical input, hydraulic actuator, electric actuator, centrifugal governor, manual control, or coordinated linkage. Upon movement, the skew cam may displace a carrier plate or other ratio-control member so as to alter the orientation or contact condition of traction rollers, planets, balls, disks, or other traction elements.

The skew cam may be arranged so that:

- a first direction of cam movement commands an upshift;
- an opposite direction commands a downshift;
- a centered position corresponds to neutral skew or no shift;
- cam displacement determines shift rate;
- cam slope determines sensitivity or mechanical advantage; and/or
- feedback cam action reduces effective skew as the desired ratio is reached.

#### 3. Carrier Plate

A carrier plate may support, guide, constrain, or transmit movement to ratio-control elements of the CVT. The carrier plate may be annular, sector-shaped, disk-like, or otherwise configured to fit within the CVT housing.

The carrier plate may include:

- slots, grooves, pins, followers, or tracks for receiving cam forces;
- mounting features for traction element supports;
- guide surfaces cooperating with a traction sun;
- coupling features for a skew cam or feedback cam;
- spring seats or preload surfaces for neutralizer assemblies;
- bearing interfaces for reducing friction during ratio change.

The carrier plate may move axially, radially, circumferentially, angularly, or in a compound path. In certain embodiments, two carrier plates may be arranged on opposite sides of traction elements, or a single carrier plate may coordinate multiple traction element supports.

#### 4. Neutralizer Assembly

A neutralizer assembly may be provided to return or bias the shift-control mechanism toward a neutral skew condition. The neutralizer assembly may include springs, torsion elements, Belleville washers, resilient pads, magnetic biasing elements, counteracting hydraulic chambers, centering detents, or paired opposing bias members.

The neutralizer assembly may act on the skew cam, feedback cam, carrier plate, actuator input, traction sun cooperating member, or an intermediate linkage. In operation, the neutralizer assembly may reduce unintended ratio drift, damp oscillations, improve ratio-hold stability, and establish a known reference position for shift control.

The neutralizer assembly may include:

- opposed springs acting on a control lever;
- a centering yoke engaging a carrier plate;
- a cam follower biased into engagement with a neutral cam recess;
- a resilient coupling between the skew cam and feedback cam;
- a preload mechanism maintaining contact between cam surfaces and followers; and/or
- an adjustable neutral position calibration feature.

#### 5. Traction Sun Cooperation

The traction sun may be a central traction member, sun roller, sun race, sun disk, or sun shaft component that cooperates with surrounding traction elements. The traction sun may participate in torque transmission and may also provide a mechanical reference or feedback signal for shift control.

A traction sun cooperating structure may include:

- a cam surface formed on or coupled to the traction sun;
- a follower engaging a sun-connected feedback ring;
- a carrier plate interface responsive to axial or angular motion of the sun;
- a linkage driven by sun movement;
- a reference member that compares sun position with commanded skew position;
- a bearing or thrust interface transmitting shift-control loads while permitting rotation.

In one embodiment, the traction sun is rotatable for torque transfer while being coupled to a non-rotating feedback member through a bearing interface. The feedback member may move axially or angularly in response to traction sun position and may actuate the feedback cam to neutralize the skew cam command.

### Representative Method of Operation

A representative method for controlling a CVT shift may include the following steps:

1. **Receiving a Shift Command**  
   A mechanical, electrical, hydraulic, centrifugal, or manual input moves a control member associated with the skew cam.

2. **Generating Skew Input**  
   The skew cam applies a displacement or force to a carrier plate or traction element support, thereby producing a skew condition in the traction elements.

3. **Initiating Ratio Change**  
   The skewed traction elements generate a ratio-changing force within the CVT, causing the traction sun and associated traction geometry to move toward a different ratio condition.

4. **Sensing Actual Ratio Position**  
   Movement of the traction sun, carrier plate, or related ratio member is transmitted to the feedback cam or to a feedback follower.

5. **Applying Negative Feedback**  
   The feedback cam generates a counteracting displacement or force that reduces the effective skew command as actual ratio approaches the commanded ratio.

6. **Neutralizing Shift Input**  
   When the target ratio is substantially reached, the feedback cam and/or neutralizer assembly returns the system to a neutral skew condition, thereby holding the selected ratio.

7. **Stabilizing the CVT**  
   The neutralizer assembly maintains preload and centering, suppresses drift, and prepares the mechanism for subsequent upshift or downshift commands.

### Optional Embodiments and Variations

In various embodiments:

- the feedback cam and skew cam may be formed as separate parts or as different cam profiles on a common body;
- the carrier plate may be a rotating member, non-rotating member, or selectively coupled member;
- the feedback cam may be driven directly by traction sun displacement or indirectly through a linkage;
- the neutralizer assembly may provide adjustable centering force;
- cam profiles may be nonlinear to match desired shift response;
- multiple followers may engage multiple cam tracks for balanced loading;
- the mechanism may include end stops for minimum and maximum ratio limits;
- friction-reducing elements may be provided between cam surfaces and followers;
- the system may be configured for mechanical-only control or for hybrid mechanical/electronic control;
- sensors may be added to detect carrier plate position, traction sun position, cam position, or ratio;
- damping elements may be added to reduce oscillation during transient shifts;
- the control system may be applied to toroidal, ball-type, roller-type, or other traction-drive CVTs.

### Potential Claim Concepts

The disclosure may support claims directed to:

1. A CVT shift-control assembly including a skew cam, a feedback cam, a carrier plate, and a neutralizer assembly arranged to control ratio change.

2. A feedback cam mechanically coupled to a traction sun to reduce a skew command as a selected ratio is approached.

3. A carrier plate movable by a skew cam and counteracted by a feedback cam to control traction element orientation.

4. A neutralizer assembly biasing a skew cam or carrier plate toward a neutral skew position.

5. A traction sun cooperating with a non-rotating feedback member through a bearing interface to provide ratio-position feedback.

6. A method of shifting a CVT by applying skew, sensing traction sun movement, feeding back ratio position through a cam profile, and neutralizing skew at a target ratio.

7. A combined cam body having a first cam profile for skew command and a second cam profile for feedback correction.

8. A cam-and-carrier arrangement configured to provide mechanical negative feedback without requiring electronic ratio sensing.

### Advantages

The disclosed arrangements may provide one or more of the following advantages:

- improved shift smoothness;
- reduced overshoot and hunting;
- mechanical ratio feedback;
- compact packaging;
- reduced need for active electronic control;
- stable ratio holding at steady state;
- improved repeatability of shift response;
- adjustable neutral position and shift sensitivity;
- simplified integration with traction sun movement;
- compatibility with multiple CVT architectures.

### Abstract

A continuously variable transmission shift-control system includes a skew cam configured to apply a ratio-changing skew command to a carrier plate or traction element support, a feedback cam configured to generate a counteracting feedback displacement responsive to traction sun or ratio-member position, and a neutralizer assembly configured to bias the system toward a neutral skew condition. The traction sun cooperates with a feedback member or cam follower to provide mechanical ratio-position feedback. During operation, actuation of the skew cam initiates a ratio change, movement of the traction sun drives the feedback cam, and the feedback cam reduces the skew command until the neutralizer assembly centers the mechanism at a desired ratio.
